          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic+over+type+theory"} logic over type theory
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theories"} dependent type theories
          %p
            Logic over dependent type theory can be considered as the concept of making explicit the metatheoretic structure of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic+with+equality"} first-order logic with equality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe+hierarchy"} universe hierarchy
            indexed by external
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/book+HoTT"} book HoTT
          %p
            The dependent type theory consisting of two levels, one level of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            for the logic and a second level of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            for the dependent type theory.
          %h2#formal_presentation Formal presentation
          %p
            It is possible to define
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definitional+equality"} definitional equality
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            in the logic layer, similar to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equality"} propositional equality
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
          %p
            For example, this presentation of dependent type theory uses a very weak
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"} predicate
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth"} truth
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjunctions"} conjunctions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjunctions"} disjunctions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implications"} implications
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantifiers"} universal quantifiers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantifiers"} existential quantifiers
            in the logic. Here,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equality"} propositional equality
            plays the role of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definitional+equality"} definitional equality
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computational+equality"} computational equality
            that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmental+equality"} judgmental equality
            played in the first presentation.
          %h3#judgments_and_contexts Judgments and contexts
          %p
            The syntax of dependent type theory can be constructed in two stages. The first is the
            %em raw
            or
            %em untyped
            syntax of the theory consisting of expressions that are readable but not meaningful. The second stage consists of defining the
            %em derivable judgements
            of the type theory inductively which then pick out the meaningful contexts, types and terms.
          %p
            A context is a list of types. Variables can be defined as De Bruijn indices in which case the type of a variable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is given by
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            One may also define contexts as coming with a variable name, in which case one needs a notion of
            = succeed "-equivalence" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
          %p Types and terms are built inductively from various constructors. Types, terms and contexts are defined mutually.
          %p We have the basic judgement forms:
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash A\; \mathrm{type}
              \-
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is a well-typed type in context
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash a : A
              \-
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              is a well-typed term of type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              in context
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} prop
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash \phi \; \mathrm{prop}
              \-
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              is a well-formed proposition in context
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} true
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash \phi \; \mathrm{true}
              \-
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              is a well-formed true proposition in context
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \; \mathrm{ctx}
              \-
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              is a well-formed context.
          %p Contexts are defined by the following rules:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} prop
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} true
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{}{() \; \mathrm{ctx}} \qquad \frac{\Gamma \; \mathrm{ctx} \quad \Gamma \vdash A \; \mathrm{type}}{(\Gamma, a:A) \; \mathrm{ctx}} \qquad \frac{\Gamma \; \mathrm{ctx} \quad \Gamma \vdash \phi \; \mathrm{prop}}{(\Gamma, \phi \; \mathrm{true}) \; \mathrm{ctx}}
          %h3#structural_rules Structural rules
          %p
            There are three structural rules in dependent type theory, the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variable+rule"} variable rule
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakening+rule"} weakening rule
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substitution+rule"} substitution rule
          %p The variable rule states that we may derive a typing judgment if the typing judgment is in the context already:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma, a:A, \Delta \; \mathrm{ctx}}{\Gamma, a:A, \Delta \vdash a:A}
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}
            be any arbitrary judgment. Then we have the following rules:
          %p The weakening rule:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi 𝒥
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi 𝒥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma, \Delta \vdash \mathcal{J} \quad \Gamma \vdash A \; \mathrm{type}}{\Gamma, a:A, \Delta \vdash \mathcal{J}}
          %p The substitution rule:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi 𝒥
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi 𝒥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash a:A \quad \Gamma, b:A, \Delta \vdash \mathcal{J}}{\Gamma, \Delta[a/b] \vdash \mathcal{J}[a/b]}
          %p The weakening and substitution rules are admissible rules: they do not need to be explicitly included in the type theory as they could be proven by induction on the structure of all possible derivations.
          %h3#definitional_equality Definitional equality
          %p Definitional equality of types and terms is formed by the following rules:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} prop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash A \; \mathrm{type} \quad \Gamma \vdash B \; \mathrm{type}}{\Gamma \vdash A \equiv B \; \mathrm{prop}}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %msub
                        %mo ≡
                        %mi A
                      %mi b
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} prop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash A \; \mathrm{type} \quad \Gamma \vdash a:A \quad \Gamma \vdash b:A}{\Gamma \vdash a \equiv_A b \; \mathrm{prop}}
          %p Definitional equality has its own structural rules: reflexivity, symmetry, transitivity, the principle of substitution, and the variable conversion rule.
          %ul
            %li Reflexivity of definitional equality
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} true
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash A \; \mathrm{type}}{\Gamma \vdash A \equiv A \; \mathrm{true}}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %msub
                        %mo ≡
                        %mi A
                      %mi a
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} true
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash A \; \mathrm{type} \quad \Gamma \vdash a:A}{\Gamma \vdash a \equiv_A a \; \mathrm{true}}
          %ul
            %li Symmetry of definitional equality
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} true
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} true
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash A \; \mathrm{type} \quad \Gamma \vdash B \; \mathrm{type} \quad \Gamma \vdash A \equiv B \; \mathrm{true}}{\Gamma \vdash B \equiv A \; \mathrm{true}}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %msub
                        %mo ≡
                        %mi A
                      %mi b
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} true
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi b
                      %msub
                        %mo ≡
                        %mi A
                      %mi a
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} true
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash A \; \mathrm{type} \quad \Gamma \vdash a:A \quad \Gamma \vdash b:A \quad \Gamma \vdash a \equiv_A b \; \mathrm{true}}{\Gamma \vdash b \equiv_A a \; \mathrm{true}}
          %ul
            %li Transitivity of definitional equality
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi C
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} true
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi C
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} true
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi C
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} true
