          %p …
          %h3#intensional_and_extensional_type_theories Intensional and extensional type theories
          %p
            In Martin-Löf dependent type theory, one makes a distinction between intensional and extensional type theories. A Martin-Löf dependent type theory is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+type+theory"} extensional type theory
            if there is an equality reflection rule where from typal equality of terms, one could derive definitional equality of terms:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %mi a
                      %msub
                        %mo =
                        %mi A
                      %mi b
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %msub
                        %mo ≡
                        %mi A
                      %mi b
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} true
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma, a:A, b:A \vdash p:a =_A b}{\Gamma, a:A, b:A \vdash a \equiv_A b \; \mathrm{true}}
          %p
            The type theory is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional type theory
            if it doesn’t have the equality reflection rule.
          %h3#propositional_reflection_and_predicate_logic Propositional reflection and predicate logic
          %p
            One could add an additional set of rules in this second presentation of Martin-Löf dependent type theory which isn’t available in the first presentation: propositional reflections. Unlike the first presentation, in this presentation, there are two notions of propositions: the external propositions and the internal propositions. The external propositions are the judgmental propositions in the logic layer of Martin-Löf dependent type theory, and correspond to the external logic. On the other hand, the internal propositions are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            equipped with a witness of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isProp"} isProp
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            and correspond to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsingletons"} subsingletons
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-propositions"} h-propositions
